Here is the root cause:
The bug arises when the function `io_wqe_create_worker()` fails to create a worker. In this scenario, the request associated with the work item is already enqueued via `io_wqe_insert_work()`, and simply calling `io_run_cancel()` to clean up the request is insufficient. This is because the request may still be executed by another worker during the cancellation process, leading to potential inconsistencies and resource management issues.

Here is the patch:

+static bool io_wq_work_match_item(struct io_wq_work *work, void *data)
+{
+	return work == data;
+}
+
